\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Dear Miguel,\
Thank you for your patience and for your detailed questions. Please find our point-by-point answers below.\
1. Which parts must be purchased again when expanding? In the attached quotation and drawings, every item marked in red is a component that must be purchased when you add further cage groups in the future. All items that are not marked can be shared and reused between the rows. In other words, when you expand you mainly need to purchase only the red-marked components; the remaining parts stay unchanged. Some equipment can be shared, but whenever you expand, a number of parts do have to be purchased \'97 I have marked these in the quotation. The same principle applies to the manure-removal system: its current cleaning capacity is matched to your current flock size, and when you add units later, only certain components need to be added.\
2. Maximum expansion for your configuration The system supports up to 90 cage groups in a 120 m house. This follows the same logic as point 1: the head (drive) section can be shared, but when you add more cage groups later, small accessories such as the egg belt will still be required.\
3. Phased expansion and civil works (the point we had not answered earlier) The capacity can be increased in phases:\
For the civil works, expansion joints are generally provided at intervals of about 6 m \'97 please confirm the exact requirement with your civil-engineering company.\uc0\u8232 We do not recommend a poultry-house length exceeding 120 m. If a longer length is required, an additional unit should be added accordingly.\u8232 When expanding in phases, please keep the foundations at the same level, so as to avoid uneven heights between units, which could affect operation.\u8232 4. Single-row layout If only one row is installed, with a walkway on each side, the total width is 4 m. The current space requirement is: length 17 m and height 4.2 m.\
Please refer to the marked-up quotation for the exact list of shared vs. additional parts. If anything needs clarifying, we will be glad to help.\
